#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082943 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc94085c-4088-5eaf-887a-eea7f040573c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Z-Score of a Given Observation"}] :question/id #uuid "3a18e6a6-e190-5977-9e1f-0d115b16c9fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5703" :question/problem "A data set has mean $\\overline{x}=15$ and variance $\\sigma^2=3.25$. Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=17.3$, giving your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz = \\frac{x-\\overline{x}}{\\sigma}\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=15\n$$\n\nand we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{\\sigma^2} = \\sqrt{3.25}\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=17.3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= (x - x^(―))/(σ) \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{17.3 - 15}{\\sqrt{3.25}} \\\\\n&\\approx 1.28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2169053d-78fc-5714-82f4-d89fc36ff695" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Observation, Standard Deviation, or Mean Given a Z-Score"}] :question/id #uuid "1c03d476-51f5-5dc4-bed6-04986f318924" :question/math-academy-id "e-5705" :question/problem "A data set has a standard deviation $\\sigma=1.2$ and an unknown mean. An observation of the data set has the value $x=13.2$ and $z$-score $z=-1.5$. Find the mean of the data set." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overline{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma$, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\overline{x} + z \\cdot \\sigma\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=13.2$, $z=-1.5$, and $\\sigma=1.2$ into the formula and solving for $\\overline{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= x^(―) + z \\cdot σ \\\\\n13.2 &= x^(―) + (-1.5) \\cdot 1.2 \\\\\n13.2 &= x^(―) - 1.8 \\\\\nx^(―) &= 13.2 + 1.8 \\\\\nx^(―) &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083037 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7e9daa2-4aaa-5c33-b611-231f3d0e4782" :knowledge-point/title "Z-Score: Word Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "68d14643-0faf-5cff-b4b9-cdb903946c39" :question/math-academy-id "e-8235" :question/problem "The mean height of a group of basketball players is $178\\textrm{cm}$. A player in this group is $184\\textrm{cm}$ tall and his $z$-score is $z = 1.5$. Find the standard deviation of the basketball players' heights." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overline{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma$, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\overline{x} + z \\cdot \\sigma\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=184$,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=178\n$$\n\nand $z=1.5$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= x^(―) + z \\cdot σ \\\\\n184 &= 178 + 1.5 \\cdot σ \\\\\n6 &= 1.5 \\cdot σ \\\\\nσ &= \\frac{6}{1.5} \\\\\nσ &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard deviation of the players heights is $4\\,\\textrm{cm}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083038 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01100ea8-2ab9-511e-8321-6ef5588c4417" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations to Compute an Unknown Mean or Standard Deviation"}] :question/id #uuid "c4f84572-0883-5fb5-b5d8-513d1aa2f50a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8236" :question/problem "A data set contains the observations $x_1=18, x_2=12$ with $z$-scores $z_1=2.8$ and $z_2=1.6$, respectively. Find the mean of the data set." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overline{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma$, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\overline{x} + z \\cdot \\sigma\n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18 &= x^(¯) + 2.8 \\cdot σ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 &= x^(¯) + 1.6 \\cdot σ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first and solving for $\\sigma$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(18 - 12) &= (x^(¯) - x^(¯)) + (2.8 - 1.6) \\cdot σ \\\\\n6 &= 1.2 \\cdot σ \\\\\nσ &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this value back into our first equation and solving for $\\bar{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18 &= x^(¯) + 2.8 \\cdot σ \\\\\n18 &= x^(¯) + 2.8 \\cdot 5 \\\\\nx^(¯) &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7e9daa2-4aaa-5c33-b611-231f3d0e4782" :knowledge-point/title "Z-Score: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "944cb518-4232-559b-96d5-fe92d13c54ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253021} {:answer/id #uuid "4601aa80-3e71-5374-bba9-6f8c4845223f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253022} {:answer/id #uuid "7fbb8e9f-0c7e-562a-b3fb-e0583283b639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253023} {:answer/id #uuid "479b44ea-9047-5c4d-8ef5-376920c2c006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253024} {:answer/id #uuid "952cfd09-0848-595e-bfd1-6a45cdf0479d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fbb8e9f-0c7e-562a-b3fb-e0583283b639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253023} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c493533-a3d2-5c20-87f6-88ebc198f6f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253020}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df832ecb-9bf5-5b15-8ef5-b4a2f30f3494" :question/math-academy-id "q-170517" :question/problem "The mean recovery time for injured runners is $\\overset{¯}{x}=70$ days with a standard deviation of $20$ days. What is the $z$-score that corresponds to a recovery time of $60$ days?" :question/worked-solution "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=60$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{¯}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{60-70}{20} \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2169053d-78fc-5714-82f4-d89fc36ff695" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Observation, Standard Deviation, or Mean Given a Z-Score"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6024899-5431-5cba-aebc-9259f96ef5d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253028} {:answer/id #uuid "fe76b7d3-1981-5f33-bed2-c0e2e4184822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253029} {:answer/id #uuid "1affc49f-1bff-5666-a1d9-9e1de9036acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253030} {:answer/id #uuid "5fdf53ef-029f-5783-9717-0726bf89d2fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253031} {:answer/id #uuid "c7375dc1-6e51-5fbc-ae21-71a1ddfc5746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6024899-5431-5cba-aebc-9259f96ef5d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253028} :answer-field/id #uuid "19955032-fd09-56f2-82a2-78394745dba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253027}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3a77fa4-fee0-58bc-8863-e33b71f55b15" :question/math-academy-id "q-17237" :question/problem "A data set has a standard deviation $\\sigma =10.5$ and an unknown mean. An observation of the data set has the value $x=35.4$ and $z$-score $z=-1.1.$ Find the mean of the data set." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=35.4,$ $z=-1.1,$ and $\\sigma =10.5$ into the formula and solving for $\\overset{―}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 35.4 & =\\overset{―}{x}+(-1.1)\\cdot 10.5 \\\\ 35.4 & =\\overset{―}{x}-11.55 \\\\ \\overset{―}{x} & =46.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc94085c-4088-5eaf-887a-eea7f040573c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Z-Score of a Given Observation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfd721f8-e1b0-57dc-9ebd-08d58e4a0866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfd721f8-e1b0-57dc-9ebd-08d58e4a0866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253035} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c4d289-d11d-518e-95bc-be0a3a0f7059" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253034}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c66cb5df-fa02-5bb5-968c-7fa0e49b7e4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-332017" :question/problem "A data set has mean $\\overset{―}{x}=4$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.25.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=3.4.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=4,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{0.25}=0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=3.4$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{3.4-4}{0.5} \\\\  & =-1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc94085c-4088-5eaf-887a-eea7f040573c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Z-Score of a Given Observation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2f7ea0b-23c2-5400-a759-8ec402880fc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2f7ea0b-23c2-5400-a759-8ec402880fc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253038} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd240eb9-5eb7-5a6c-bf95-1e4b8f1721c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253037}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "228cc6f3-1808-5165-94f7-09b330f1f3c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-332018" :question/problem "A data set has mean $\\overset{―}{x}=7$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1.7.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=6.2,$ rounding your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=7,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{1.7}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=6.2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{6.2-7}{\\sqrt{1.7}} \\\\  & ≈-0.61,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc94085c-4088-5eaf-887a-eea7f040573c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Z-Score of a Given Observation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff465509-e151-58d7-af35-68de76fffbc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.59" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff465509-e151-58d7-af35-68de76fffbc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.59" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253041} :answer-field/id #uuid "6291258d-3f41-5b6c-8881-0e47a2c2bd58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253040}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "387cd435-3846-537c-8dbc-b4fbba04f51d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332024" :question/problem "A data set has mean $\\overset{―}{x}=-1.2$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.32.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=-2.1,$ rounding your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=-1.2,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{0.32}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=-2.1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{-2.1-(-1.2)}{\\sqrt{0.32}} \\\\  & ≈-1.59,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2169053d-78fc-5714-82f4-d89fc36ff695" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Observation, Standard Deviation, or Mean Given a Z-Score"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a34250-6060-5154-be0e-418e556f9cb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14a34250-6060-5154-be0e-418e556f9cb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253044} :answer-field/id #uuid "16413860-6879-5ac8-95ec-a5dd6c7e36b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253043}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "330c58a6-167a-5927-8488-a9c368002278" :question/math-academy-id "q-332033" :question/problem "A data set has a standard deviation $\\sigma =2.5$ and an unknown mean. An observation of the data set has the value $x=11$ and $z$-score $z=0.8.$ Find the mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ of the data set.\n\n$\\overset{―}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=11,$ $z=0.8,$ and $\\sigma =2.5$ into the formula and solving for $\\overset{―}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 11 & =\\overset{―}{x}+0.8\\cdot 2.5 \\\\ 11 & =\\overset{―}{x}+2 \\\\ \\overset{―}{x} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2169053d-78fc-5714-82f4-d89fc36ff695" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Observation, Standard Deviation, or Mean Given a Z-Score"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b7835d7-6985-579c-a75b-2629635c5ada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b7835d7-6985-579c-a75b-2629635c5ada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253047} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b3d0569-b81e-5762-84aa-c02470a2f6a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253046}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26bebcb6-0fc4-55f8-8377-c7bba785f83f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332047" :question/problem "A data set has a mean $\\overset{―}{x}=10$ and an unknown standard deviation. An observation of the data set has the value $x=12.1$ and $z$-score $z=1.05.$ Find the standard deviation of the data set.\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=12.1,$ $\\overset{―}{x}=10,$ and $z=1.05$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 12.1 & =10+1.05\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 2.1 & =1.05\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2169053d-78fc-5714-82f4-d89fc36ff695" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Observation, Standard Deviation, or Mean Given a Z-Score"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20507df9-b2ce-5562-85d0-d7af8c6882a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20507df9-b2ce-5562-85d0-d7af8c6882a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253050} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5affb72-7c6d-54fa-b613-74d83e2e19d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253049}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e36ba23-9627-5e17-aefa-a4e6e5a83ed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-332211" :question/problem "A data set has a mean $\\overset{―}{x}=-0.3$ and a standard deviation $\\sigma =0.5.$ An observation of the data set has a $z$-score $z=1.4.$ Find the value $x$ of this observation.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\overset{―}{x}=-0.3,$ $z=1.4,$ and $\\sigma =0.5$ into the formula and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ x & =-0.3+1.4\\cdot 0.5 \\\\ x & =-0.3+0.7 \\\\ x & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01100ea8-2ab9-511e-8321-6ef5588c4417" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations to Compute an Unknown Mean or Standard Deviation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6088d170-501b-583e-8cf3-e3f735ef1911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6088d170-501b-583e-8cf3-e3f735ef1911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253053} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a5d33e6-4075-50a0-8f93-0b3ab4d62ff1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253052}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aae1f933-06ba-565d-aaa7-cd1446205445" :question/math-academy-id "q-332233" :question/problem "A data set contains the observations ${x}_{1}=-1.2,{x}_{2}=2.8$ with $z$-scores ${z}_{1}=-0.5$ and ${z}_{2}=2,$ respectively. Find the mean $\\overset{¯}{x}$ of the data set.\n\n$\\overset{¯}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n-1.2=\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.5)\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n2.8=\\overset{¯}{x}+2\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.8-(-1.2) & =(2-(-0.5))\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 4 & =2.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this value back into our first equation and solving for $\\overset{¯}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1.2 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.5)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ -1.2 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.5)\\cdot 1.6 \\\\ -1.2 & =\\overset{¯}{x}-0.8 \\\\ \\overset{¯}{x} & =-0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01100ea8-2ab9-511e-8321-6ef5588c4417" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations to Compute an Unknown Mean or Standard Deviation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10833697-a385-5ae8-92aa-d97d1c578694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.94" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253056} {:answer/id #uuid "d1090a83-422d-5de2-a634-12d4721babc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1090a83-422d-5de2-a634-12d4721babc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253057} :answer-field/id #uuid "19e41c37-8f6b-5472-9b41-ea3035bbe3a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253055}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "962ed8d5-3136-5afc-a515-af1dd7e097fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-332238" :question/problem "A data set contains the observations ${x}_{1}=-6.24,{x}_{2}=-1.64$ with $z$-scores ${z}_{1}=-0.8$ and ${z}_{2}=1.2,$ respectively. Find the mean $\\overset{¯}{x}$ of the data set.\n\n$\\overset{¯}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n-6.24=\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.8)\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n-1.64=\\overset{¯}{x}+1.2\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1.64-(-6.24) & =(1.2-(-0.8))\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 4.6 & =2\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this value back into our first equation and solving for $\\overset{¯}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6.24 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.8)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ -6.24 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.8)\\cdot 2.3 \\\\ -6.24 & =\\overset{¯}{x}-1.84 \\\\ \\overset{¯}{x} & =-4.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01100ea8-2ab9-511e-8321-6ef5588c4417" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations to Compute an Unknown Mean or Standard Deviation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3bad2cc-eb52-5adf-ba37-5b32b5d4efc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3bad2cc-eb52-5adf-ba37-5b32b5d4efc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253060} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ebc4966-7ba0-5a45-89f6-d25ab003986d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253059}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e64b9681-ce05-59b7-b509-eed464a28ad6" :question/math-academy-id "q-332239" :question/problem "A data set contains the observations ${x}_{1}=3.08,{x}_{2}=8.68$ with $z$-scores ${z}_{1}=-1.2$ and ${z}_{2}=2.3,$ respectively. Find the mean $\\overset{¯}{x}$ of the data set.\n\n$\\overset{¯}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n3.08=\\overset{¯}{x}+(-1.2)\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n8.68=\\overset{¯}{x}+2.3\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8.68-3.08 & =(2.3-(-1.2))\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 5.6 & =3.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this value back into our first equation and solving for $\\overset{¯}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.08 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-1.2)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 3.08 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-1.2)\\cdot 1.6 \\\\ 3.08 & =\\overset{¯}{x}-1.92 \\\\ \\overset{¯}{x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01100ea8-2ab9-511e-8321-6ef5588c4417" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations to Compute an Unknown Mean or Standard Deviation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3684371e-62c1-5a94-b9b0-b59633ed9826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253063} {:answer/id #uuid "a04dff67-7930-52c4-9678-868600de5b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a04dff67-7930-52c4-9678-868600de5b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253064} :answer-field/id #uuid "4053961a-ccb4-57cf-a6b2-eba4b3948c19" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253062}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2740c50b-5c9f-5ee1-8ec5-a13a1b5ce0ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-332248" :question/problem "A data set contains the observations ${x}_{1}=0.5,{x}_{2}=4.5$ with $z$-scores ${z}_{1}=-3$ and ${z}_{2}=5,$ respectively. Find the standard deviation $\\sigma $ of the data set.\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n0.5=\\overset{¯}{x}+(-3)\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n4.5=\\overset{¯}{x}+5\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4.5-0.5 & =(5-(-3))\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 4 & =8\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7e9daa2-4aaa-5c33-b611-231f3d0e4782" :knowledge-point/title "Z-Score: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb6843b2-aba3-5ca6-8e2b-898290c639d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb6843b2-aba3-5ca6-8e2b-898290c639d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253067} :answer-field/id #uuid "8577a4f2-3422-52ad-9aa3-e882c4465382" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253066}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "365c4459-b953-5858-96dc-1cda9c95f096" :question/math-academy-id "q-332254" :question/problem "The mean score on a national mathematics contest is $\\overset{¯}{x}=80$ points with a standard deviation of $5$ points. What is the $z\\text{-score}$ that corresponds to a student's score of $65$ points?\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=65$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{¯}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{65-80}{5} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7e9daa2-4aaa-5c33-b611-231f3d0e4782" :knowledge-point/title "Z-Score: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2bf24a8-fb46-511e-ba8e-19fe8162dfc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2bf24a8-fb46-511e-ba8e-19fe8162dfc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253070} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ec31e32-7a9a-513f-83ff-89322270293d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253069}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1dd8a745-3186-5132-97bd-1381104984bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-332257" :question/problem "The mean heart rate of a group of patients during light exercise is $\\overset{¯}{x}=70$ beats per minute. A particular patient in this group has a heart rate of $74$ beats per minute, and his $z$-score is $z=2.5.$ Find the standard deviation of the patients' heart rates in the group.\n\nThe standard deviation of the heart rates is\n{{field-1}}\nbeats per minute." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\overset{―}{x}=70,$ $z=2.5,$ and $x=74$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 74 & =70+2.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 4 & =2.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{4}{2.5} \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard deviation of the patients' heart rates in the group is $1.6$ beats per minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc94085c-4088-5eaf-887a-eea7f040573c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Z-Score of a Given Observation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f6442b8-3dac-5784-8697-c28a4fd9998b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253073} {:answer/id #uuid "0373ecd7-a713-5501-9dcc-9fb43d79a741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253074} {:answer/id #uuid "2d11fefc-4b0b-5192-9a22-6d1922f7c366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253075} {:answer/id #uuid "c325b34e-5a42-5d44-9fcb-f6eff3d2c0b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253076} {:answer/id #uuid "1c3dd489-4f7b-548f-bcc7-80627d5105be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f6442b8-3dac-5784-8697-c28a4fd9998b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253073} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb82b16b-6758-5142-a87b-345d298408d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253072}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3ffce57-1bac-5c81-b423-d3a26144e3ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-94457" :question/problem "A data set has mean $\\overset{―}{x}=6$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=10.8.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=10.5,$ giving your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=6,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{10.8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=10.5$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & ≈\\frac{10.5-6}{\\sqrt{10.8}} \\\\  & ≈1.37\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc94085c-4088-5eaf-887a-eea7f040573c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Z-Score of a Given Observation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9013badc-4266-5796-8ad1-accf800e931a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253080} {:answer/id #uuid "21b7df4a-97f6-58d8-99f1-77d60cd3aeae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253081} {:answer/id #uuid "7f14e462-b10c-5709-b41b-7812a2b55b26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253082} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6a6a16-c7cc-52d1-85e4-dcdeceb21f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253083} {:answer/id #uuid "46c3e1ca-06ca-59e6-b4a9-dab888cc429d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f14e462-b10c-5709-b41b-7812a2b55b26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7.38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253082} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ab03f41-3c62-5e4d-a946-c1dcee2c1aff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253079}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44c8409c-8d74-5d20-842c-c5884ee92a67" :question/math-academy-id "q-94460" :question/problem "A data set has mean $\\overset{―}{x}=28$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=3.60.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=14,$ giving your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=28,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{3.60}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=14$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & ≈\\frac{14-28}{\\sqrt{3.60}} \\\\  & ≈-7.38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2169053d-78fc-5714-82f4-d89fc36ff695" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Observation, Standard Deviation, or Mean Given a Z-Score"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "812f445c-882d-5016-a569-c0a4728e5f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253087} {:answer/id #uuid "13de8b1d-e15c-5132-9339-2ae9e117c80a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253088} {:answer/id #uuid "2886bcd4-4d29-5fe6-8bc3-a33414e36bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253089} {:answer/id #uuid "870b759a-8c08-53d4-8d5a-7491b42ec2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253090} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a092e2-7406-57df-90a0-391242c504cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13de8b1d-e15c-5132-9339-2ae9e117c80a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253088} :answer-field/id #uuid "f51136db-35df-514a-80c7-05619fb85c15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253086}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82197dba-cfec-5a33-8238-e0bc37ae14dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-94471" :question/problem "A data set has a mean $\\overset{¯}{x}=14.5$ and an unknown standard deviation. An observation of the data set has the value $x=20$ and $z$-score $z=2.2.$ Find the standard deviation of the data set." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=20,$ $\\overset{¯}{x}=14.5,$ and $z=2.2$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 20 & =14.5+2.2\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 5.5 & =2.2\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01100ea8-2ab9-511e-8321-6ef5588c4417" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations to Compute an Unknown Mean or Standard Deviation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f83bda0-2a37-5502-9c6c-7f33969aea46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253094} {:answer/id #uuid "6cab8794-42b3-5346-ad3b-b05c98284ec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253095} {:answer/id #uuid "b0570b6f-cf00-566e-9922-edbb2d89d561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253096} {:answer/id #uuid "1d224498-762a-5e61-8c15-71995b255010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253097} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1e0f21-64e3-54ab-9e76-b943708f1611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cab8794-42b3-5346-ad3b-b05c98284ec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253095} :answer-field/id #uuid "631453a2-733e-53c3-90b8-2540cfd57eef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253093}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d7baf40-c071-58b2-9d36-657822e7ff04" :question/math-academy-id "q-94475" :question/problem "A data set contains the observations ${x}_{1}=28,{x}_{2}=37$ with $z$-scores ${z}_{1}=0.8$ and ${z}_{2}=1.2,$ respectively. Find the standard deviation of the data set." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+0.8\\cdot \\sigma .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+1.2\\cdot \\sigma .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(37-28) & =(1.2-0.8)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 9 & =0.4\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =22.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7e9daa2-4aaa-5c33-b611-231f3d0e4782" :knowledge-point/title "Z-Score: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baa3524a-ecd7-5498-b8a1-664d224cb597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253101} {:answer/id #uuid "3eaa3cc4-4ad7-576a-a85b-e1836857c25e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.0\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253102} {:answer/id #uuid "0b54f4bb-1c40-51ec-8cbf-16b9a107e56d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253103} {:answer/id #uuid "e21359d4-427e-5120-9b37-fffe6a7a05d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.2\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253104} {:answer/id #uuid "27378599-e318-5d19-90b8-470801cf9ca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27378599-e318-5d19-90b8-470801cf9ca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253105} :answer-field/id #uuid "08d47a6b-ce31-52d9-a089-f62202f25e3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253100}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d4c2dcb-2073-5bfd-816d-d45085c526dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-94519" :question/problem "The mean weight of a group of ballet dancers is $45\\text{ kg}.$ One of the dancers in the ballet group weighs $38\\text{ kg}$, and her $z$-score is $-2.5.$ Find the standard deviation of the weight of the dancers in the ballet group." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=38,$ $\\overset{―}{x}=45,$ and $z=-2.5$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 38 & =45+(-2.5)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ -7 & =-2.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{-7}{-2.5} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the standard deviation of the ballet dancer's weights is $2.8\\,\\text{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7e9daa2-4aaa-5c33-b611-231f3d0e4782" :knowledge-point/title "Z-Score: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5fdef25-43a2-5ea9-86ad-b6fe023cdc53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253108} {:answer/id #uuid "b17d946d-d57f-586d-a64f-b284f7f31792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253109} {:answer/id #uuid "a3570b3b-a816-52aa-ac25-13ed4796f39e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253110} {:answer/id #uuid "58988327-5cb7-5874-9e47-c16b98cc20de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253111} {:answer/id #uuid "f1204690-5b44-5c40-8961-d55ea051ce4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1204690-5b44-5c40-8961-d55ea051ce4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253112} :answer-field/id #uuid "99d50a3e-f3ae-5fea-8c23-c829a5f71644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253107}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60a38f7c-dfa0-5d09-92e4-d758707ad857" :question/math-academy-id "q-9785" :question/problem "The mean weight of a group of newborn children is $3.2kg$ with a standard deviation of $350g.$\nWhat is the $z$-score that corresponds to a weight of $3kg?$ Give your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $350g=0.350kg$\n\nThe $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{¯}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3.2}{0.350} \\\\  & =-0.57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nto $2$ decimal places."}]}
